On Sat, 2024-07-06 at 09:52 +0200, Peter Hilber wrote:
> On 05.07.24 18:17, David Woodhouse wrote:
> > On Fri, 2024-07-05 at 10:12 +0200, Peter Hilber wrote:
> > > On 03.07.24 12:18, David Woodhouse wrote:
> > > > On Wed, 2024-05-22 at 19:00 +0200, Peter Hilber wrote:
> > > > > +VIRTIO_RTC_LEAP_PRE_POS resp.\ VIRTIO_RTC_LEAP_PRE_NEG pre-annou=
nce a
> > > > > +positive resp.\ negative leap second. Pre-announcing starts at t=
he
> > > > > +earliest at the 17th day of the month including the leap second.
> > > >=20
> > > > Where does 17th come from? Is there an existing precedent for that?
> > > >=20
> > >=20
> > > It doesn't come from anywhere. I set it to the second half of the mon=
th to
> > > disambiguate pre-announcing, in case a particular smearing algorithm =
would
> > > only start smearing on the day after the leap second (at the start of=
 the
> > > month).
> >=20
> > The day *after* the leap second shouldn't be considered "pre-
> > announcement", should it? Wouldn't that be another case for the
> > VIRTIO_RTC_LEAP_POST_{POS,NEG} that I requested? It is notification of
> > a leap second which occurred in the recent past, for which smearing
> > activity may still be ongoing.
>=20
> The SHM would always indicate true UTC time so the `leap` state there wou=
ld
> be LEAP_PRE / LEAP / LEAP_POST according to the leap second.
>=20
> Would you want to indicate the occurrence of the actual leap second also
> when doing the smearing for the VIRTIO_RTC_REQ_READ_CROSS message? I
> thought to just indicate the time when smearing is being done.

I think that just indicating the time when smearing is being done is
sufficient.

That tells the consumer when the returned time is not accurate UTC and
any calibration of a counter is going to experience some deliberately
induced frequency 'error', and that's what's important.

If we're putting the onus on userspace to cope, we might reasonably
expect userspace to know exactly *how* its apparent counter frequency
is going to jump, e.g. by ~12PPM at the start of smearing, and back
again in the opposite direction at the end.

The main thing I want to ensure we think about is a guest=C2=A0which is
powered up in the moments after midnight, when smearing is still being
done (in the case of virtio-rtc exposing a smeared clock) or still
*needs* to be done (in the case of the guest doing the smearing for
itself).

In that case, the guest may not have seen the pre-announcements of the
leap second which is now supposed to be historical. But still needs to
consume the clock information accordingly, until the smearing period
ends.

So exposing a SMEAR flag whenever the clock is actually being smeared
is fine, and also a POST_POS and POST_NEG flag to allow the guest to do
so when the clock it's being given is true UTC and has already stepped.


